 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of steps per ray. Performance scales linearly with step count [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the volume texture, where $V$ is the number of voxels. Accumulators use constant memory per pixel [S9].
-*   **Performance:** Fixed step counts provide predictable performance [S5, unofficial]. Early ray termination significantly speeds up rendering of opaque or dense volumes [S1, S9].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; handles sparse and implicit data well via variable steps [S3]; real-time capable on GPUs [S1][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Biased estimates if standard quadrature is used without delta tracking [S4]; low resolution causes blockiness [S10, unofficial]; early termination may miss subtle transparent details [S9].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time volume rendering, NeRF, SDF rendering [S6, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When physically accurate light transport (scattering) is required without approximation [S4]; when exact surface boundaries in binary data are needed without compositing .
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of steps per ray [S5, unofficial]. Performance scales linearly with the number of steps [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for the volume texture, where $V$ is the number of voxels [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Fixed-step ray marching is predictable and GPU-friendly due to uniform loop lengths [S5, unofficial]. Adaptive ray marching (e.g., sphere tracing) can be significantly faster in sparse volumes by skipping empty space [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, supports real-time performance on GPUs, handles implicit and procedural volumes naturally [S1][S5, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed-step methods can be aliasing-prone or inefficient in sparse volumes [S3]. Early termination can miss subtle transparent details [S9]. Low simulation resolution causes blocky artifacts [S10, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time volume rendering, neural graphics (NeRF), SDF rendering [S6, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When exact physical accuracy is required for heterogeneous media with complex scattering (use Monte Carlo or delta tracking) [S4].
 
 ## Background
-Ray marching was introduced as an efficient method to solve the volume rendering equation with real-time performance, notably used in systems like Jrender [S1]. It is a core component of GPU-based volume rendering techniques that perform ray marching in fragment shaders [S2, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in rendering Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) [S6, unofficial]. Variants like sphere tracing were introduced for antialiased rendering of height fields and extended to general implicit volumes [S3].
+Ray marching was introduced as an efficient method for volume rendering, extending the concept of ray casting to iterative sampling [S1]. It is widely used in modern differentiable rendering libraries like Jrender [S1] and GPU-based implementations [S2, unofficial]. The technique is fundamental to rendering Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) [S6, unofficial]. Variants like sphere tracing were introduced for antialiased rendering of height fields and extended to general implicit volumes [S3].
 
